<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>dev.vexelray.framework</groupId>
        <artifactId>vexelray-framework</artifactId>
        <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>vexelray-framework-template</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>vexelray-framework-template</name>
    <description>
        The project builder: reads a template, works out the tree it would write, refuses the files it
        should not, and writes them transactionally. Its output is this framework's primary witness —
        see docs/architecture.md, "The witness is the project builder, not an application".
    </description>

    <!--
      Arrived here from mainframe-template, where the boundary this module needs was already drawn.
      Two packages lived there; one of them had no business being in a terminal:

        dev.mainframe.template        the engine. "Imports nothing from MainFrame, and nothing from
                                      anywhere else either: a JDK and a folder of templates is the whole
                                      of it." That is this module.
        dev.mainframe.template.shell  the adapter — slots to form fields, answers to a record, the
                                      result to a Plan. Stays there, and becomes a caller of this.

      The test that pom asked of the first package was "could another project use it", and it answered
      itself: "Scaffold and Checks would drop into an installer or a Maven mojo unchanged." This is that
      happening. Nothing in the move needed a change of shape — only the package name and the classpath
      root the bundled templates sit under.

      JDK-only, and for the same reason -api and -core are: a thing that computes a tree of bytes needs
      no terminal, no GPU and no siblings installed to be tested. junit is the only dependency, and
      `mvn -o test` runs the whole module on a bare checkout.
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
